  "query_preview": "/Thursday through Monday/...\nIssued 302 AM MDT MON AUG 31 2026\nDeterministic and ensemble consensus remains strong for a deep \nlong-wave trough and closed low pressure system to stall near \nthe Oregon and Washington coastline through much of the long \nterm period. This positioning keeps our forecast area situated \nin persistent, moderately unstable southwesterly flow aloft. \nEmbedded shortwaves will periodically orbit the main low center,\nmaintaining persistent shower and thunderstorm chances through \nThursday and Friday, particularly over southeast Oregon and the \nWest Central Idaho Mountains. SW Idaho valleys will remain \nsusceptible to dry slotting, though isolated activity cannot be \nruled out as atmospheric instability pulses each afternoon.\n\nA substantial cooling trend will take hold starting Thursday as\nlower heights overspread the area. Temperatures will fall\nroughly 5 to 10 degrees below seasonal averages, holding valley\nhighs in the mid to upper 70s through the upcoming weekend and\ninto Monday. Afternoon wind gusts will remain elevated daily,\nfavoring typical wind-prone corridors including southeast Oregon\nand terrain south of the Snake River Plain. The wide variations\nin precipitation among the ensemble clusters is due to the\npotential entrainment of tropical moisture from the hurricane\nforecast to form off the coast of Baja California over the next\n48 hours. While the confidence in precipitation amounts is low,\nhigh confidence exists for a prolonged cool, windy, and\nperiodically unsettled pattern through Monday.",
